<h2>The case and its history</h2>
<p>The FIR began elsewhere: heroin was recovered from three co-accused, and the investigation led a raiding team on 18 September 2024 to the house the petitioner shared with his wife. The prosecution alleged recovery of 260 grams of heroin concealed in a polythene bag inside a pair of shoes. Both spouses were arrested; a supplementary chargesheet followed in March 2025; charges under Sections 21(c), 25 and 29 of the NDPS Act were framed in July 2025; and the trial stood at the stage of prosecution evidence.</p>
<p>The petitioner's first approach to the High Court had ended in withdrawal — his affidavit of antecedents was inadequate — with liberty to refile. The trial court dismissed his bail application in September 2026, relying on the commercial quantity, the rigours of Section 37 and his fourteen prior cases. The renewed application before the High Court succeeded.</p>

<h2>The three pillars of the order</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Parity.</strong> The co-accused wife, arrested from the same premises in respect of the same recovery, had bail since 7 April 2026. The court applied the same considerations to the petitioner.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>A genuinely borderline quantity.</strong> At 260 grams gross — wax bag included — against a 250-gram threshold, the net weight was not clearly established on the record. The foundation for Section 37's rigours was therefore shaky.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Custody without an end in sight.</strong> More than two years in custody with the trial far from concluding engaged the settled principle that prolonged incarceration of an undertrial erodes the justification for continued detention.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">
<p>Bail was granted on a personal bond of Rs. 50,000 with one surety, coupled with conditions: regular attendance at trial, no departure from the NCT of Delhi without leave, monthly reporting to the SHO, and no contact with witnesses. The court recorded that nothing in the order reflects on the merits.</p>
</div>

<h2>Why the weight of the packaging matters</h2>
<p>The Supreme Court has long required that the quantity attributed to an accused be determined with care, and bail courts routinely confront seizures that hover around a statutory threshold. The difference is not academic: below the commercial line, bail is governed by ordinary principles; above it, Section 37 demands reasonable grounds to believe the accused is not guilty. Where the gross weight includes packaging material and the net weight is unclear, the benefit of that uncertainty at the bail stage goes to the accused — the prosecution cannot claim the stricter regime on a record that does not clearly establish its factual premise.</p>
<p>Equally instructive is the treatment of antecedents. Fourteen prior cases is a formidable list, but the court looked at outcomes rather than entries: none had ended in conviction, and apprehensions about conduct on bail were met with conditions rather than refusal. The order thus reflects both strands of modern bail jurisprudence — scepticism toward incarceration as a default, and confidence in tailored conditions as the instrument of control.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>In NDPS defence, obtain and scrutinise the seizure memo, FSL report and weighment record: whether the recorded weight is gross or net, and what the packaging weighed, can decide which bail regime applies.</p>
<p>Document the custody period and the pace of trial precisely — number of witnesses examined against those cited is the arithmetic courts respond to.</p>
<p>Where a co-accused with a comparable role has bail, plead parity with specifics: date of the order, the role ascribed to each accused, and the absence of distinguishing features.</p>
<p>File a complete and candid affidavit of antecedents; an inadequate affidavit cost this petitioner months.</p>
</div>
